  "query_preview": "..A large upper low over the northwest United States\nwill linger through the weekend, with ridging to our east. This\nsetup keeps south-southwesterly flow over our region which will\nallow for an increase in moisture starting later today and through\nSunday. While precipitable water values will increase above normal\nfor early September, instability does not look high as much of the\nmoisture is in the mid/upper levels bringing clouds and limiting\nsurface heating. Some of this moisture, especially by Sunday in \nnorthwest AZ, is getting pulled northward from Hurricane Marie \nlocated around 700 miles west of the tip of the Baja peninsula. \nFor today, mainly just isolated showers or thunderstorms are \nexpected, with the vast majority of locations remaining dry. Later\ntonight through Sunday, the deeper moisture will move into \nnorthwest Arizona and portions of NV/UT but again instability is \nlacking and much of the activity may just be light to moderate \nshowers. The main band of precipitation has also continued to \ntrend farther westward and is now forecast by much of the guidance\nto be from southern CA to Las Vegas and into UT, possibly just \nbrushing far northwest Coconino County where there is around a 40%\nchance of rain Sunday.\n\nMonday - the northwest US low is forecast to shift eastward\nallowing for some significantly drier air to move into our area\nfrom the west. Storm chances will be limited to areas along the\nAZ/NM line with dry weather elsewhere. Starting Tuesday and\ncontinuing through next week - an elongated ridge covering most of\nthe southern US will gradually shift westward. The flow around\nthis ridge looks favorable for monsoon moisture to shift back into\nour region. Storm chances increase to 30-50% each afternoon from\nWednesday onward, highest for the mountains. Temperatures should \nbe near normal for early September.",
